  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry & seasonable weather continues through mid-week \nwith a good amount sunshine. Temperatures range from the the 30s-\nlower 40s overnight to upper 50s in the afternoons. \n\nDry westerly flow continues through the middle of the week under \nzonal flow at the mid levels, sandwiched between a surface low over \neastern Canada and high pressure over the southeast U.S. Not much \nchange in the airmass overhead with 925 mb temps in the 6-8C range \nTue/Wed. Some lingering moisture in the mid/upper levels moves \nthrough early leading to increased cloudcover this morning, but \ndrying out/clearing up by the afternoon with plenty of sun. This \ncontinues Wednesday though we'll start to see some \nmoisture/increasing cloudcover filtering in later in the day \nWednesday. Altogether, should see high temperatures each day topping \nout in the mid to upper 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring another storm Friday, although there\nremains high uncertainty in exact timing and storm track.\n\nThe latest round of ensemble guidance continues to show large \nuncertainty in the development of a potential storm as we head into \nthe late week/weekend, especially within the track of the low. While \nall guidance continues to indicate paths anywhere from Maryland to \nNova Scotia, the ECMWF and Canadian ensembles (and their AI \nensembles) have trended towards the 40N/70W benchmark or slightly \nsouth while the GFS ensemble shows tracks closer towards the \nsouthern New England coast. Despite the uncertainty in track, most \nguidance has indicated the low deepening into the mid 990mbs by \nearly Friday morning as well as 850mb temperatures dropping to below \nfreezing across southern New England by Friday evening, supporting \nchances for some snow to mix into the region Friday and Friday \nnight across the higher elevation regions.\n\nProbabilities continue to indicate only a 30-50% chance for 1\"+ of \nsnow in the higher terrain, with the highest chances along the \neastern slopes of the Berkshires, and less than 20% probability for \n3\" or more. As mentioned by previous forecasters, those probabilites \nassume a 10:1 snow to liquid ratio, and unless we get really heavy \nprecipitation rates (highly unlikely), those 10:1 SLR accumulations \nwould likely be lower with wetter snow characterization being more \nfavored. Any winter weather impacts in the higher elevations are \nlikely to be very limited to nil, and this is reflected by low \nprobabilities of Minor winter storm impacts in the Probabilistic \nWinter Storm Severity Index.\n\nHeading into the second half the weekend, the low will push further \ninto Atlantic Canada and high pressure begins to build over the Mid-\nAtlantic region, bringing a return to blustery and dry conditions to \nsouthern New England for the start of the next work week.",
